# Incident 005 — CI/CD Pipeline Failure (Missing Credentials)

| Field | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Incident** | CI/CD pipeline failed — registry credentials not configured |
| **Date** | [fill in run date] |
| **Severity** | Medium — release blocked, no impact to the running application |
| **Symptoms** | Pipeline fails at the Docker push (or deploy) stage with an authentication/unauthorized error; earlier stages (install, test, build) pass normally |
| **Impact** | No new image could be pushed to the registry; deployment of the current change was blocked until fixed. Already-running pods were unaffected |
| **Detection** | Pipeline stage shown as Failed in CI; job log shows `unauthorized: authentication required` (or connection refused) on `docker login` / `docker push` |
| **Investigation** | Checked the job log first — confirmed the login step itself was failing. Checked Settings > CI/CD > Variables and found the registry username, password, and host/IP were never added |
| **Root Cause** | The registry credentials (username, password, and registry host/IP) were never added to the project's CI/CD Variables when the pipeline was set up, so `docker login` had nothing to authenticate with |
| **Resolution** | Added the three missing values as CI/CD Variables: `REGISTRY_USER`, `REGISTRY_PASSWORD` (masked), and `REGISTRY_HOST` (the registry IP/endpoint) |
| **Verification** | Re-ran the pipeline; `docker login` and `docker push` stages completed successfully; image visible in the registry with the correct tag |
| **Preventive Action** | Added an early **credential pre-check** stage that fails fast with a clear message if any of `REGISTRY_USER` / `REGISTRY_PASSWORD` / `REGISTRY_HOST` is empty, instead of letting the pipeline run through build/test before failing at push; documented the required variables in `README.md` |
| **Lessons Learned** | A pipeline that references a variable which was never created still runs — it just fails at the step that needs it, several stages in. Required CI/CD variables should be checked at the very start of the pipeline, not discovered by a failed push |

---

## Reproduction steps

```yaml
# .gitlab-ci.yml (relevant stage)
docker_push:
  stage: docker-build
  script:
    - echo "$REGISTRY_PASSWORD" | docker login -u "$REGISTRY_USER" --password-stdin $REGISTRY_HOST
    - docker build -t $REGISTRY_HOST/employee-app:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA .
    - docker push $REGISTRY_HOST/employee-app:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA
```

```bash
# Induce: trigger the pipeline before any registry credentials exist in CI/CD Variables
git commit --allow-empty -m "trigger pipeline with no registry credentials set"
git push origin main
# Pipeline runs; docker_push stage fails: "unauthorized: authentication required"
# or "dial tcp: no such host" if REGISTRY_HOST is also blank

# Investigate
# - Open the failed job log, confirm the login step is the one failing
# - Settings > CI/CD > Variables: confirm REGISTRY_USER / REGISTRY_PASSWORD / REGISTRY_HOST are missing

# Fix: add the credentials
# Settings > CI/CD > Variables > Add variable
#   REGISTRY_USER      = <registry username>
#   REGISTRY_PASSWORD  = <registry password>   (masked)
#   REGISTRY_HOST      = <registry IP / endpoint>

# Verify
git commit --allow-empty -m "re-run pipeline after adding registry credentials"
git push origin main
# Confirm docker_push stage: Passed
docker pull $REGISTRY_HOST/employee-app:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA
```

> **Evidence to attach:** the failed job log showing the authentication error, the CI/CD Variables page before (empty) and after (three values added), and the passing pipeline run once credentials are in place.